#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Consolidation & Fuel Planning",
  unit: "Cross Country Endorsement | Lesson 4",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
To consolidate dead reckoning and pilotage on a complete, self-prepared flight plan, and to calculate, plan and manage fuel throughout the flight.
]


,overview: [
This lesson addresses elements *1.1 Prepare flight plan* (determine fuel requirements including hold and reserve), *1.3 Navigate aircraft* (monitor fuel use and revise fuel planning if required), and consolidates *1.8 Use of electronic devices* by having the student operate an EFB as a cross-check to DR. Fuel planning is conducted with assistance from the instructor: the student must understand fuel terminology and the legal requirements for fuel carriage (RAAus FOM and CASA AC 91-15), calculate fuel required, and maintain an in-flight fuel log. The route should suit the student's progress — ideally complex, with airspace requiring pilotage and some featureless terrain. When the student is ready (which may be after more than one lesson), the instructor demonstrates a *diversion* on the final leg (preview of Lesson 5) and the student then flies that leg using the instructor's numbers to see how accurate they are. This lesson directly addresses the competency variables *knowledge of a refuelling procedure at an alternate aerodrome* and *two landings at different locations during a navigation exercise*, and may be repeated until the standard is met.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Define fuel terminology: trip, taxi, contingency, holding, final reserve, fuel required and margin.
- State the legal fuel requirements for the flight (RAAus FOM; CASA AC 91-15, CASR Part 91).
- State the legal requirements with regard to choosing an alternate aerodrome (CASR Part 91.235, MOS 7.02, MOS 8.04, MOS 8.02 )
- Explain the considerations for refuelling at an alternate/foreign aerodrome.
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Prepare a complete nav log: track, heading, ETI, fuel and frequencies.
- Calculate fuel required (including hold and reserve) and confirm endurance against the route.
- Maintain an in-flight fuel log; manage tank selection as required.
- Operate the EFB as a cross-check while continuing to navigate by DR.
- Conduct arrival and a touch-and-go at a foreign aerodrome without assistance.
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [TEM] Maintain a continuous "Plan B": nearest suitable aerodrome and fuel/daylight margins.
- [TEM] Apply ADM to fuel decisions (revise plan if burn is higher than planned).
- [HF] Manage cockpit organisation and EFB storage/mounting.
- [SA] Maintain SA across a more complex route with airspace and featureless terrain.
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- Fuel calculations *(Pg 146)*: fuel consumption rate and start-up/taxi allowance *(Pg 146–147)*; fixed reserve, margin fuel and reserves *(Pg 147)*; safe endurance *(Pg 148)* — refer FOM for RAAus specifics
- Alternate aerodromes and holding requirements *(Pg 117)*
- Flight plan and allowing for the climb *(Pg 172–173)*
- Vertical navigation — altimetry: altimeter *(Pg 175–177)*, QFE *(Pg 178)*
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 75 mins \
Equipment required: VNC, ERSA, plotter, E6B, EFB, nav log, AC 91-15, current GAF/GPWT and NOTAM \

*Non-Technical Skills* (5 minutes)
- [TEM] Continuous Plan B; fuel and daylight margins.
- [TEM] ADM for fuel: trigger points to divert or land for fuel.
- [HF] EFB mounting, storage and cockpit management.

*Fuel planning* (20 minutes)
- Fuel terminology; RAAus FOM and AC 91-15 requirements; calculating fuel required including hold and reserve.

*Diversion planning* (20 minutes)
- Requirement for alternate aerodrome with a TAF, as per CASR Part 91 regulations; determining holding requirements.

*In-flight fuel management* (15 minutes)
- Fuel log, tank management, periodic "fuel remaining/endurance/nearest aerodrome" checks.
- Requirements when fuel reaches critical levels.

*Refuelling at an alternate* (10 minutes)
- ERSA refuelling availability; payment/access; planning for a fuel stop.

*Diversion preview* (10 minutes)
- The diversion method (hold/draw/check/estimate/turn/time/CLEAROFF/refine) to be demonstrated on the final leg; full development next lesson.
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor*]
- Review weather, NOTAM and fuel independently; select a route matched to the student's ability.
- Confirm the student's complete nav log before flight.
- Assist the student in fuel planning, including holding, alternate and reserve fuel.
- Prepare to demonstrate a diversion to the home aerodrome on the final leg.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student*]
- Complete IMSAFE; brief the go/no-go decision.
- Prepare a complete nav log (TR, HDG, ETI, frequencies and airport information).
- With instructor assistance, calculate fuel required including holding, alternate and reserve.
- Identify a refuelling option at the planned arrival aerodrome and brief it.
- Discuss the importance of a Plan B (leads into diversions).
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Demonstration*]
- Final-leg diversion to the home aerodrome: hold, draw route, *check route*, estimate heading, turn onto heading, estimate time, CLEAROFF, refine with plotter and 1 in 60.
- Cross-checking the DR picture against the EFB.
#text(size:12pt)[*Student Practice*]
- Navigate the route largely unassisted; instructor intervenes only as needed and does not allow the student to get lost.
- Maintain an accurate fuel log; answer instructor prompts on fuel remaining, endurance and nearest aerodrome.
- Conduct arrival and a touch-and-go at a foreign aerodrome without assistance.
- Fly the instructor's diversion numbers on the final leg and compare actual vs estimated.
- Maintain straight-and-level ±150 ft and heading ±5°.
]

,common_errors:[
- *No features for CTA/SUA clearance*: When on DR within 5 miles of a boundary, there *must* be a pre-identified feature to locate the boundary.
- *Messy record keeping*: Fuel log needs to be kept legible and organised. Same with diversion information. 
- *EFB becomes primary*: Keep DR/pilotage primary; the EFB is a cross-check (v8 §1.8).
- *No Plan B*: Always know the nearest suitable aerodrome.
- *Track crawling*: Establish position relative to the desired route, make a correction, then leave the chart alone.
- *CLEAROFF forgotten on diversion*: CLEAROFF is a fallback to make sure all tasks for the diversion are completed.
]

,post_flight:[
- *Student Self-Assessment*: What went well; how close the instructor's diversion numbers were and why. Did you need any assistance during the flight with standard navigation?
- *Instructor Feedback* (10 min):
  - Evaluate against the standards below (S / C / NC).
  - Highlight strengths; nominate focus areas.
  - Discuss TEM/ADM (eg. fuel decision points, Plan B).
- *Scenario Reflection*: "If a headwind raised your burn by 10%, at which waypoint would you decide to divert for fuel?"
- *Next Steps*: Assign homework. See below.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- Uses 1-in-60 effectively; revises ETA (when required) to within ±3 minutes.
- Position-fixes quickly and accurately.
- Maintains straight-and-level ±150 ft and heading ±5°.
- Operates the nav log on standard flights (no diversions) and manages the fuel log and frequencies.
- Recalls and implements CLEAROFF independently.
- Arrives at, and conducts a touch-and-go at, a foreign aerodrome without assistance.
- Demonstrates knowledge of a refuelling procedure at an alternate aerodrome.
- *Competency stage*: this lesson is the natural pre-solo-navigation consolidation. Repeat as required until S is achieved. Record S / P / C / NC.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Ceiling/visibility comfortably above VFR minima; conditions suited to a longer/complex route.
- *Altitude*: Suitable VFR cruise level clear of CTA steps; minimum 1,500 ft AGL en route.
- *Emergency Procedures*: Maintain awareness of landing areas; keep fuel and daylight margins in mind.
- *TEM*:
  - Do not allow the student to get lost — this is a consolidation, not a lost-procedure lesson.
  - Maintain a buffer from CTA/PRD across the more complex route.
  - Monitor traffic at both arrival aerodromes.
- *Suggested Routes*: YHEC-GON-DBO-YWSG (TGL)-YCAB-GON-YHEC | YHEC-CPMN-BBI-YCDR (TGL)-GON-YHEC
- *Aircraft Limits*: Operate within POH/AFM; manage fuel tank selection per the POH.
]

,homework:[
- Read Bob Tait's RAAus Cross Country Endorsement:
  - Fuel calculations (Pg 146–148); alternate aerodromes and holding requirements (Pg 117); flight plan and allowing for the climb (Pg 172–173).
  - Review RAAus FOM fuel provisions and AC 91-15.
- Prepare for next lesson (*Diversions & Lost Procedure*): read establishing a DR position and area of probability (Pg 187–189) and reading from the ground to the map (Pg 190).
- Practice: complete a full fuel plan (including hold and reserve) for a route with a fuel stop.
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
